As far as I know, [`ActivatedRoute.params`](https://angular.io/docs/ts/latest/api/router/index/ActivatedRoute-interface.html#!#params-anchor) is indeed changed before routing is actually complete. You can subscribe to the actual router events using [`Router.events`](https://angular.io/docs/ts/latest/api/router/index/Router-class.html#!#events-anchor), and wait for [`NavigationEnd`](https://angular.io/docs/ts/latest/api/router/index/NavigationEnd-class.html). This seems to produce the results you are looking for. See the updated plunker [here](http://plnkr.co/edit/Rfc7J5OmkRKhTrvlhXWB?p=preview).

```
this._router.events.subscribe(event => {
  if (event instanceof NavigationEnd) { 
    // this._activatedRoute.snapshot is up to date
  }
});
```

If it isn't a bug then we have to have the proper way to handle this, but I didn't see the way. Let we need to get `params` and `data` together at a some place in code. 
First we try this:
````
   this.route.data.subscribe(data=>{
       const params = this.route.snapshot.params;
       //Now we have data and params together 
   });
````
but `params` are not current and let it be as is. 
Because of `data` derives from `params` (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/12767#issuecomment-260493205) we try doing:
````
   this.route.params.subscribe(params=>{
       const data = this.route.snapshot.data;
       //Now we have data and params together again
   });
````
But again `data` from last route.

Ok, let try to write: 
````
Observable.merge(this.route.data, this.route.params).bufferCount(2).subscribe(result=>console.log(result));
````
It is works but not properly because for first time we have `data` in `result[0]` but for second time `data` is swapped with `params` and now `data` are in `result[1]`. 

See https://plnkr.co/edit/NR2ZfY1H7n3XzMJZi89s?p=preview

That was true for the cases when we try changing route we are on it:
````
[
    {
        path:'',
        component:Main
    },
    {
        path:'sibling',
        component:Sibling
    },
    {
   
      path: 'parent',
      component: Parent, //in this place we try to change route to ./a 
      children:[
        {
          path: ':child',
          component: Child
          resolve:{
            data:Resolver
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
````
 But if we try changing route from `sibling` to `parent`  then all will be right.
Will be waiting for fixing of this bug.

If someone knows the way to get `data` and `params` in strong fashion I will appreciate that.

@vicb I have made some investigations, 
first, after redirects and all passed checks we create new router state:
https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/2.3.1/modules/@angular/router/src/router.ts

Then at:
https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/2.3.1/modules/@angular/router/src/create_router_state.ts#L30
we start to recursively recreate a new tree

And `createNode()` function does this:
https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/2.3.1/modules/@angular/router/src/create_router_state.ts#L26
````
if (prevState && routeReuseStrategy.shouldReuseRoute(curr.value, prevState.value.snapshot)) {
    const value = prevState.value;    
    value._futureSnapshot = curr.value;
    const children = createOrReuseChildren(routeReuseStrategy, curr, prevState);
    return new TreeNode<ActivatedRoute>(value, children); //!!!problem is in here 
}
....
````
That is why each new node gets old value `prevState.value`.
Then this new state is used in:
https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/2.3.1/modules/@angular/router/src/router.ts#L744

And finally we have it in `advanceActivatedRoute(this.futureState.root)` as a future state.
Maybe the simple fix will resolve it but it's need more time to explore all router in details.
The fix is:
````
//    const value = prevState.value;    
    value._futureSnapshot = curr.value;
    const children = createOrReuseChildren(routeReuseStrategy, curr, prevState);
    return new TreeNode<ActivatedRoute>(curr.value, children); 
````
But maybe the problem is in applied strategy, why we goes to `routeReuseStrategy.shouldReuseRoute = true` branch in `createNode()`.

@olegdunkan 
> There is no way to substitute ReuseStrategy via ExtraOptions

u can provide your own `RouteReuseStrategy`. just `{provide: RouteReuseStrategy, useClass: CustomReuseStrategy}`

@DzmitryShylovich thanks, it works. 
 
For now I have substituted only one method 
````
@Injectable()
export class CustomReuseStrategy implements RouteReuseStrategy {
 ...
 shouldReuseRoute(future: ActivatedRouteSnapshot, curr: ActivatedRouteSnapshot): boolean {
    return false;
  }
}
````
Now the problem has gone, see
https://plnkr.co/edit/BOoZobkwdYvFeorFT6vN?p=preview

But of course it's the temporary solution, because I think the default reuse strategy has to work.

Even if the thread is old, I found it searching for an answer at same problem.

The solution is simple because route.params detect the value changed of a parameter and you can call the action into route subscriber. For me, works fine in constructor.

```
constructor(private route: ActivatedRoute) {
route.params.subscribe(routeParam => {
      this.id = routeParam['id'];
      // do something
    });
}
```

Try to not call the method "do something" in ngOnInit, because this will be called two times.

